Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-28
Subject: Pass FillWord a word count, not a byte count, when zeroing new2oldreg
License: expat

`compiler/i386/csopt386.pas:433` calls

  fillword(new2oldreg, sizeof(new2oldreg), RS_INVALID);

`new2oldreg' is `TRegArray = array[TRegEnum] of tsuperregister', and
`tsuperregister = type word' (cgbase.pas:165) -- so `sizeof(new2oldreg)'
is twice its length-in-words. fpc's `FillWord(var x; count: SizeInt;
Value: Word)' takes `count' in words, so the call writes 2x the array
size and overflows the stack frame's `p_startreginfo' slot into
neighbouring locals. fpc-native bootstraps tolerated this because
nothing bounds-checks the write; an AddressSanitizer-instrumented
stage1 (e.g. tp2cc's C++ output) trips on it immediately while
compiling the linux RTL.

Pass the correct word count.

--- a/compiler/i386/csopt386.pas
+++ b/compiler/i386/csopt386.pas
@@ -430,7 +430,7 @@
   with startRegInfo do
     begin
       newRegsEncountered := [RS_EBP, RS_ESP];
-      fillword(new2oldreg,sizeof(new2oldreg),RS_INVALID);
+      fillword(new2oldreg,sizeof(new2oldreg) div sizeof(tsuperregister),RS_INVALID);
       new2OldReg[RS_EBP] := RS_EBP;
       new2OldReg[RS_ESP] := RS_ESP;
       oldRegsEncountered := newRegsEncountered;
